<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stepan Razin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1630–<a href="/years/1671">1671</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Stenka Razin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stepan Razin was a Don <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> leader who raided the Caspian coasts of Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 1668–1669 and in 1670 led a great rebellion of Cossacks and peasants along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> against the Russian tsardom. After defeat at Simbirsk, Don Cossack elders handed him over, and he was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1671.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stepan-razin.jpg" alt="Stepan Razin" width="320" height="328" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Пауль Фюрст, вдова и наследники · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stepan_Razin,_German_portrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Razin's father, Timofey Razya, is said to have come from a suburb of Voronezh, and the identity of his mother is debated. He first appears in the sources in 1652 and, after six years without a trace, reappeared as the leader of a band that levied tribute from vessels on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. His elder brother Ivan was executed in 1665 for deserting the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>. Wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the Russo-Swedish War had raised taxes and conscription, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> had been fixed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobornoe-ulozhenie">Law Code of 1649</a>, and many peasants fled south to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1667 Razin led a small group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> from the Don down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> to the Caspian, aiming to set up a base at Yaitsk, which he took in July 1667 by posing as a pilgrim. In 1668 he defeated the voivode Yakov Bezobrazov and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> for about eighteen months. He failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> killed roughly 400 of his Cossacks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a>, but he pillaged Farahabad and in July 1669 destroyed a Persian fleet off Suina. In August 1669 he accepted a pardon from Tsar Alexei Mikhailovich at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>.</p><p>In 1670 he rebelled openly, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/starocherkasskaya">Cherkassk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Tsaritsyn</a> and sailing down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> with almost 7,000 men. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a>, the streltsy, let him in, and he killed those who opposed him and set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> republic. He moved up the Volga, taking Saratov and Samara, but Simbirsk held out, and on 1 and 4 October his army was beaten there by Yuri Baryatinsky. His envoys had stirred up areas as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>, Tambov and Penza, but the Don Cossacks turned against him after the Patriarch anathematized him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1671 Razin and his brother Frol were captured at Kagalnik by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> elders and handed to the tsar's officials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Razin was quartered on the scaffold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a> on 16 June 1671. The historian Paul Avrich notes that the repression of the revolt was far more brutal than the insurgents' own atrocities. Razin had revolted against the traitor boyars rather than the tsar, and the revolt ended in greater government control and some loss of Cossack autonomy.</p><p>He became a figure of folklore and song. A poem by Dmitry Sadovnikov of 1883, set to music, tells of him throwing a captured Persian princess into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>; modern historians doubt the episode, which two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> travellers report in different forms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stenka Razin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Urban unrest, as in the Salt Riot of 1648 and the Copper Riot of 1662, and the great Cossack and peasant revolt of <b>Stepan Razin</b> in 1670–1671, showed deep social tensions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The government remembered the risings of <b>Stenka Razin</b> and Yemelyan Pugachev and kept the hosts loyal with land and privileges.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander Glazunov wrote a piece on Balakirev's tune in 1905 and used it for a theme of his symphonic poem <b>Stenka Razin</b>, and Max Bruch made it the last movement of his Suite on Russian Folk Melodies.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</a></li><li><blockquote>The Don also bred great rebellions, led by <b>Stepan Razin</b> in 1670 and Kondraty Bulavin in 1707, which Moscow crushed.</blockquote><a href="/state/don-cossack-host">Don Cossack Host</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1656" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Stepan Razin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1656">What was here in 1656? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
